AB  - This article reviews recent advances in multisensory emotion induction based on virtual reality (VR) technology and electroencephalography (EEG), focusing on its applications in emotion recognition and mental health. By combining multi-sensory stimuli (e.g., vision, hearing, touch, and temperature changes), VR technology creates highly immersive situations that effectively enhance the realism and intensity of emotional experiences. The introduction of EEG technology further improves the accuracy of emotion recognition, providing scientific support for real-time monitoring of user emotional responses and optimisation of emotional regulation. The article systematically reviews the design of multi-sensory interaction, experimental methods for inducing emotions, and their practical application in psychological intervention. It also analyses the current challenges and shortcomings of research and looks ahead to future research directions. The results of the review show that multi-sensory emotion induction based on VR and EEG is of great value in the fields of emotion management, psychotherapy and education, and provides a theoretical basis and practical reference for the development of personalised emotion regulation technology.
ER  - 

