AB  - This study investigated the impact of a segmented in-class flipped model compared to a traditional pre-class flipped model on learning outcomes within an intellectual property design course integrated with high-immersion virtual reality. A quasi-experimental non-equivalent control group design was employed involving 33 undergraduate students majoring in digital media arts. Participants were assigned to either a traditional flipped classroom with 16 students or a segmented in-class flipped classroom with 17 students. Data were collected via assessments measuring knowledge retention and flow experience alongside cognitive load and design confidence. Results indicated a distinct pedagogical trade-off where the traditional group demonstrated superior consolidation of declarative knowledge while the inclass group experienced a marginal retention regression despite a stronger academic baseline. This phenomenon is termed performance inversion. The in-class group conversely reported significantly higher flow states. Furthermore a confidence reversal effect was observed where the experimental group overcame a lower baseline to achieve significantly higher post-task design confidence grounded in both visual decoding and process fluency. The findings suggest that high-immersion environments impose a retention tax on rote memory while paying a dividend in psychological engagement. Consequently instructional choice must align with specific educational objectives where traditional models suit terminology retention and segmented in-class models foster creative intuition and psychological readiness.
ER  - 

