AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) is reshaping how animated visual presentations are conceived, produced, and experienced, extending visual effects (VFX) workflows from linear timelines into adaptive, data-driven systems. This review synthesizes research and practice on AI adoption in animation and virtual reality (VR), emphasizing effects authoring, neural rendering, previsualization, compositing, and audience-facing interaction. We examine how AI-augmented interfaces accelerate ideation, enable embodied camera and staging decisions inside immersive workspaces, and support real-time evaluation of lighting, particles, volumetrics, and compositing cues using learned denoisers and neural approximations of offline renders. The manuscript is framed as a review and conceptual analysis rather than an empirical study. It maps how AI supports prediction, reconstruction, and constrained generation in VFX pipelines, while also identifying constraints that complicate deployment, including hallucinated detail, model bias, temporal inconsistency, data interoperability with established digital content creation pipelines, intellectual-property and provenance risks, and the need for repeatable, version-controlled generation. Practical mitigation strategies are summarized, including hybrid VR-desktop workflows, constraint-based generation, lightweight proxy assets, standardized export paths to offline renderers, and model/seed logging for reproducibility. The review concludes with research directions on perceptual metrics, collaborative multiuser editing with AI agents, and physically based real-time neural rendering for next-generation VFX in animation.
ER  - 

