AB  - Studies have shown that Augmented Reality (AR) is an effective tool for improving workers' efficiency in manual assembly and maintenance processes. However, creating high-quality AR instructions remains a significant challenge due to the high development costs, complex assembly procedures, and the continuously evolving nature of instructional materials. To address these challenges, we propose using Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) to automatically generate AR assembly instructions from PDF-based manuals and present them in a tablet-based assembly assistant application. This approach leverages the content generation capabilities of Large Language Models (LLMs) and the adaptability of RAG to produce rich AR instructions that include text, images, animations, and 3D models without requiring labor-intensive manual intervention or technical expertise. The system was evaluated by 20 experts from Siemens, each performing a complete assembly of an Industrial Automation System (IAS). The generated AR instructions consistently outperformed PDF-based manuals in both the NASA Task Load Index (NASA-TLX) and System Usability Score (SUS) while reducing task completion time and error rates of workers in a real-world assembly scenario. This demonstrates that the AR assembly instructions generated by our RAG pipeline significantly improve workers' efficiency, highlighting the potential of a combined approach for scalable AR guidance across industrial domains.
ER  - 

