AB  - This study explores how Extended Reality (XR) and artificial intelligence (AI) - supported motion tracking can be integrated into computer graphics education. The approach combines lectures, hands-on exercises, and team projects so that students not only study the theory of transformations, rendering, and animation but also apply it in practice. Using XR headsets together with markerless tracking models—such as CNNs and Transformer-based pose estimators—students worked with 3D content in real time and controlled game engines through gestures. Over two academic years, experiments with undergraduate groups showed clear benefits: students were more motivated, collaborated more effectively, and developed stronger technical skills. The results indicate that XR and AI- supported motion tracking can make abstract topics easier to grasp while preparing learners for professional roles in interactive media, gaming, and virtual design.
